<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lament · Sacred · 1842 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Va, pensiero (Chorus of the Hebrew Slaves)</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="it">Va, pensiero</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A chorus from Verdi's opera Nabucco (1842), sung by Hebrew exiles in Babylon who long for their lost homeland.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Temistocle Solera</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Giuseppe Verdi</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Italian opera audiences of the Risorgimento era; later Italians at large</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Milan, Italy</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1842 (premiere of Nabucco at La Scala)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lament · Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Temistocle Solera (Nabucco, 1842; he died in 1878))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/va-pensiero">Va, pensiero</a>&quot;, also called the Chorus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> Slaves, is a chorus from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-verdi">Giuseppe Verdi</a>'s opera Nabucco, first staged in 1842. It recalls the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-captivity">Babylonian captivity</a> after the destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a> in 586 BC: the exiled Hebrews sing of their homeland. The libretto is by Temistocle Solera, who drew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psalm-137">Psalm 137</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-verdi">Verdi</a> wrote Nabucco at a hard moment in his life: his wife and children had all just died of illness. Despite a reported vow to give up opera, he was under contract to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-scala">La Scala</a>, and its director, Bartolomeo Merelli, pressed the libretto on him. At home Verdi happened to open it at &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/va-pensiero">Va, pensiero</a>&quot; and, he later said, heard the words singing. At the first rehearsal the stagehands cheered and beat their tools on the floor and the sets. Verdi later said that this was the opera with which his artistic career really began, and the opera with its chorus established him as a major composer in 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>Some scholars have held that the chorus was meant as an anthem for Italian patriots seeking to unify the country and free it from foreign rule in the years up to 1861, the exiles' longing for a lost homeland being thought to speak to many Italians. Some modern scholars reject this, seeing no link between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-verdi">Verdi</a>'s operas of the 1840s and 1850s and Italian nationalism, except for some passages of I Lombardi (1843). Other recent work, including Philip Gossett's study of the choruses of the 1840s, suggests that this revisionist view may have gone too far in dismissing the political meaning of &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/va-pensiero">Va, pensiero</a>&quot;.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-verdi">Verdi</a> died, onlookers in the streets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> began singing &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/va-pensiero">Va, pensiero</a>&quot; as his funeral procession passed. A month later, when he was reburied beside his wife at the Casa di Riposo, the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arturo-toscanini">Arturo Toscanini</a> conducted a choir of 820 singers in it.</p><p>On 27 January 1981 the journalist Giorgio Soavi proposed, in a letter published by Indro Montanelli in Il Giornale, that &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/va-pensiero">Va, pensiero</a>&quot; replace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s national anthem. The idea was discussed and dropped, then taken up again without result by Senator Umberto Bossi in 2009; Bossi's party, Lega Nord, has adopted it as its official hymn and sings it at all party meetings. In 2011, after conducting the chorus during Nabucco at the Teatro dell'Opera in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, Riccardo Muti made a short speech against cuts to Italy's arts budget and asked the audience to sing it with the chorus in support of culture.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrews</a>, captive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, send their thoughts home to the country they have lost and pray for the strength to endure. The mood is a slow, solemn longing for home, sung by the whole people as one voice.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Italian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Italian</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Va, pensiero, sull'ali dorate;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Go, thought, on golden wings;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">va, ti posa sui clivi, sui colli,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>go, alight on the slopes, on the hills,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">ove olezzano tepide e molli</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>where, warm and soft, there breathe their fragrance</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">l'aure dolci del suolo natal!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the sweet breezes of the native soil!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Del Giordano le rive saluta,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Greet the banks of the Jordan,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">di Sionne le torri atterrate.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the toppled towers of Zion.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">O, mia patria, sì bella e perduta!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O my homeland, so beautiful and lost!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">O, membranza, sì cara e fatal!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O remembrance, so dear and fatal!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Arpa d'or dei fatidici vati,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Golden harp of the prophetic seers,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">perché muta dal salice pendi?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>why do you hang mute from the willow?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Le memorie nel petto raccendi,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Rekindle the memories in our breast,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">ci favella del tempo che fu!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>speak to us of the time that was!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">O simile di Sòlima ai fati</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Either, like the fates of Solyma,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">traggi un suono di crudo lamento,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>draw forth a sound of harsh lament,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">o t'ispiri il Signore un concento</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>or may the Lord inspire in you a harmony</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">che ne infonda al patire virtù!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that may instil in us strength to endure suffering!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Temistocle Solera, Nabucco (1842); as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Va%2C_pensiero" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (9)</summary><ul><li>The source file names Edward Downes as translator; this English is a new line-by-line translation of the Italian, not his.</li><li>line 1.3-4: literally 'where are fragrant, warm and soft, / the sweet airs of the native soil'; 'aure' are breezes or airs.</li><li>line 2.4: 'membranza' is a poetic form of 'rimembranza' (remembrance, memory); 'fatal' is 'fateful' as well as 'deadly', the memory bound up with their fate.</li><li>line 3.1: 'vati' are poet-prophets (Latin vates); 'fatidici' means prophetic, foretelling fate. The harp hanging on the willow recalls Psalm 137.</li><li>line 3.3-4: 'raccendi' and 'favella' are imperatives addressed to the harp; 'ci' is 'to us'.</li><li>line 4.1: 'Sòlima' is Solyma, an old poetic name for Jerusalem. The phrase 'O simile di Sòlima ai fati' is compressed: 'either, like [one who sings] the fates of Jerusalem' or 'either, in keeping with Jerusalem's fate'; readings differ.</li><li>line 4.2: 'crudo' is raw, harsh or bitter.</li><li>line 4.3: 'concento' is a harmony or concord of sounds.</li><li>line 4.4: 'virtù' here is strength or fortitude (the power to bear 'il patire', suffering); 'ne' is 'in us'.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
